### Context 

Our TAG (Training Assessment Group) team has been to build an interactive dashboard that allows users to explore FitBit data. This project forms part of the DATA2007 team working skills assessment and is a practice for the Assessed Group Project (AGP) in January 2027.

The dataset comes from 30 FitBit users and includes activity, heart rate, sleep and movement information. For this project, we treat them as a group of athletes who are friends and want to see how everyone is exercising.

### Project Idea

Our Dashboard is designed around the concept of a fitness platform for a group of friends where users shall be able to:

- Create an account and join a friendship group
- Compare their performance with friends
- Track progress over time
- Spot their own exercise habits and trends

This Fitbit dataset acts as a prototype dataset representing users who have signed up to the platform and connected their devices. The dashboard shows the platform could function by allowing exploration of the sample data as though the users belonged to a shared friendship group.

The idea comes from a simple problem: people who exercise with friends want an easy way to compare activity, see trends and stay motivated through shared progress.

**Scope:** Account creation and group joining are part of the platform concept, but the prototype only covers exploring the sample data.

### Target Users 

The primary users of the dashboard are athletes who are interested in tracking their activity within friendship groups. In the future, the same platform could support people just starting their fitness journey, or anyone who wants a personalised view focused on their own specific goals.

### Data Sources

The dataset was collected through a survey on Amazon Mechanical Turk between 3 and December 2016. Thirty FitBit users consented ro share their data. It is provided as 18 CSV files, each covering a different measurement type.

#### Questions the dashboard should answer 

The dashboard shall help users answer questions such as:

- Who is the most active member of the group?
- How do activity levels vary between users?
- What days are people most active?
- What exercise patterns show up across the group?
- How do heart rate and calorie expenditure differ between users?

#### What the dashboard provides 

- Visualisations of activity data
- Side-by-side comparisons between users
- Personal and group performance metrics
- Insights into exercise habits and behaviour

